{"id": "2026-09-07-news-afd-wins-saxony-anhalt-election", "date": "2026-09-07", "stream": "news", "headline": "AfD wins Saxony-Anhalt election", "summary": "The far-right AfD won Germany's Saxony-Anhalt state election on 6 September with about 43.9%, far ahead of the CDU's 17.2%, but every rival rules out governing with it.", "url": "https://wahlergebnisse.sachsen-anhalt.de/wahlen/lt26/", "source_domain": "wahlergebnisse.sachsen-anhalt.de", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics", "politics"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "The far-right AfD (Alternative für Deutschland) won Germany's Saxony-Anhalt state election on Sunday 6 September by a wide margin, taking about 43.9% of the vote — roughly 39 of the 87 seats — against 17.2% for the governing CDU (Christian Democratic Union), according to the state returning officer's official preliminary count. It is the AfD's strongest result yet in any German state and more than doubles the CDU's share in a region the CDU had led since reunification. The SPD (9.3%), Greens (8.9%), Left (8.6%) and the new left-populist BSW (5.3%) trailed far behind. AfD co-leader Alice Weidel called the outcome \"a clear mandate\" to form a government and predicted Chancellor Friedrich Merz would be gone by Christmas — but every other party in the state parliament rules out governing with the AfD, which Germany's domestic-intelligence agency classifies as a \"confirmed right-wing extremist\" organisation, so despite finishing first the AfD has no realistic path to a governing majority. Coalition talks among the mainstream parties are only now beginning.", "why": "a far-right party clearing 40% in a German state is a threshold event for post-war German politics and a direct blow to Merz's federal CDU nine months into his chancellorship; it sharpens the debate over the \"firewall\" that has kept the AfD out of power and reverberates across a Europe already watching hard-right gains.", "thread_id": "2026-09-05-news-afd-poised-to-top-saxony-anhalt-vote", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-05", "event_date": "2026-09-06", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["AfD", "Saxony-Anhalt", "CDU", "Alice Weidel", "Friedrich Merz"]}
{"id": "2026-09-07-news-israeli-strikes-kill-11-in-south-lebanon", "date": "2026-09-07", "stream": "news", "headline": "Israeli strikes kill 11 in south Lebanon", "summary": "Israeli air strikes on Kfar Reman in southern Lebanon early on 7 September killed at least 11 people, including two children and two medics, despite the 2024 truce.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/9/7/israeli-air-attack-kills-at-least-10-people-in-southern-lebanon", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Israeli air strikes on the town of Kfar Reman in southern Lebanon early on Monday 7 September killed at least 11 people, including two children and two medics, and wounded several more, Lebanon's official National News Agency (NNA — the state wire service) reported. A warplane hit a residential building, killing nine there, while further strikes struck the wider Nabatieh district. It is the deadliest single day in a run of Israeli strikes on southern Lebanon over the past month — the NNA-reported toll of these incidents has climbed from around four killed on Sunday 6 September to seven in mid-August and now eleven — all carried out despite the November 2024 ceasefire that formally ended the Israel–Hezbollah war. Israel says it targets Hezbollah fighters and infrastructure; Monday's casualties, which Lebanese authorities describe as civilians and rescue workers, were not independently reconciled with that account.", "why": "the strikes keep eroding a truce that is nominally still in force, and the mounting civilian toll raises the risk of the fragile Lebanon front reopening.", "thread_id": "2026-08-06-news-israel-strikes-south-lebanon-amid-rome-talks", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-06", "event_date": "2026-09-07", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Israel", "Lebanon", "Hezbollah", "Kfar Reman"]}
{"id": "2026-09-07-news-von-der-leyen-visits-greenland-with-eu-package", "date": "2026-09-07", "stream": "news", "headline": "Von der Leyen visits Greenland with EU package", "summary": "Von der Leyen arrived in Nuuk on 6 September for a two-day visit, pledging a substantial EU investment package as Trump presses for US control of the Danish Arctic territory.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/von-der-leyen-in-greenland-with-eu-investments-amid-trump-pressure/a-79109197", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics", "politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "European Commission President Ursula von der Leyen arrived in Greenland's capital Nuuk on Sunday 6 September for a two-day visit, pledging a \"substantial\" EU investment package as US President Donald Trump keeps pressing for American control of the Danish Arctic territory. She was received by Greenlandic Prime Minister Jens-Frederik Nielsen and Danish Prime Minister Mette Frederiksen, and the package — expected to run to hundreds of millions of euros — targets critical minerals, satellite communications and renewable energy, alongside a promise of closer political ties. Greenland, home to about 57,000 people, left the then-European Community in 1985 but its residents remain EU citizens through Danish nationality. Trump has repeatedly said the US \"needs\" the mineral-rich island for national security, citing — without evidence, DW notes — a supposed Chinese and Russian naval presence; both Copenhagen and Nuuk have rejected his demands.", "why": "the visit is a pointed European counter to Washington's annexation talk, testing whether the EU can offer Greenland enough to keep an increasingly contested Arctic aligned with Europe rather than under US pressure.", "thread_id": "2026-09-07-news-von-der-leyen-visits-greenland-with-eu-package", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-07", "event_date": "2026-09-06", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["European Union", "Greenland", "Ursula von der Leyen", "Denmark", "Donald Trump"]}
{"id": "2026-09-07-news-zelensky-sees-war-lasting-into-winter", "date": "2026-09-07", "stream": "news", "headline": "Zelensky sees war lasting into winter", "summary": "Zelensky said on 7 September the war looks set to continue into winter after US envoys Witkoff and Kushner's shuttle diplomacy produced no announced breakthrough.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/ukraine-zelensky-says-war-looks-set-to-go-on-winter/live-79135930", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky said on Monday 7 September that Russia's war looks set to continue into the winter, after the latest round of US-led diplomacy produced no announced breakthrough. His comments followed talks in Kyiv with US envoys Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner, who had met President Vladimir Putin at the Kremlin two days earlier; the Kremlin called that meeting \"constructive,\" but the shuttle mission ended without a ceasefire or a concrete framework. Meanwhile Russian regional officials said Ukrainian drone strikes killed at least two people and injured several in the Perm and Belgorod regions overnight.", "why": "it is the clearest signal yet that the Witkoff–Kushner initiative — the most active US peace push in months — has stalled, leaving the front line and the strikes on both sides unchanged as a third winter of full-scale war approaches.", "thread_id": "2026-09-07-news-zelensky-sees-war-lasting-into-winter", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-07", "event_date": "2026-09-07", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Ukraine", "Russia", "Volodymyr Zelensky", "Steve Witkoff", "Vladimir Putin"]}
{"id": "2026-09-07-news-nepal-mourns-1-300-flood-dead", "date": "2026-09-07", "stream": "news", "headline": "Nepal mourns 1,300 flood dead", "summary": "Nepal held a national day of mourning on 7 September for the more than 1,300 people killed in the 26 August Himalayan glacial-lake flood, as bodies were still being recovered.", "url": "https://www.letemps.ch/articles/le-nepal-observe-un-jour-de-deuil-national-treize-jours-apres-la-catastrophe", "source_domain": "letemps.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Nepal held a national day of mourning on Monday 7 September for the more than 1,300 people killed in the Himalayan glacial-lake flood that struck on 26 August, as search teams kept recovering bodies 13 days on. Buddhist monks performed rituals for the dead in the hard-hit Nuwakot district, and many victims are being buried in mass graves — a practice that has distressed relatives still waiting for the thousands reported missing to be identified. The toll has risen steadily since the disaster, from about 270 in the first days to over 1,000 by early September and past 1,300 now, with rescue and recovery work continuing across the flood zone.", "why": "it is among the deadliest disasters in Nepal's recent history, and the scale of the loss — driven by a glacial-lake outburst of the kind climate scientists warn is becoming more frequent in a warming Himalaya — is only now becoming fully clear.", "thread_id": "2026-08-27-news-himalayan-glacier-collapse-floods-kill-270", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-27", "event_date": "2026-08-26", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Nepal", "Nuwakot"]}
